{
  "id": "Compound::DB01036 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Tolterodine, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, is shown to bind to Chrm4, which is involved in the activation of G-protein coupled receptors. This binding leads to the participation of Chrm4 in the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity and muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity pathways, ultimately resulting in the participation of Chrm1. The evidence suggests that Tolterodine exerts its effects by modulating the activity of Chrm4, which in turn influences the function of Chrm1 through G-protein signaling. The consistent binding to Chrm4 and the involvement of Chrm1 in these pathways provide a mechanistic basis for the potential therapeutic effect of Tolterodine on muscarinic receptors. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Chrm4 as a key mediator in the interaction between Tolterodine and Chrm1, supporting a direct and biologically plausible relationship between the two entities.",
